### Need more help?

* If you still get a 401/403 error, double-check that **no `mapbox://` URLs** remain in your style JSON (sources, sprite, glyphs).
* If you’re using **Mapbox-specific plugins** (e.g., `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`), replace them with the MapLibre equivalents (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`, `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`, etc.).
* For a deeper dive on the callback-to-promise migration, see the **“Call-backs became promises (GL JS v4.0.0)”** section of the migration guide.

Feel free to paste the exact snippet you’re using if you run into any other errors!

Below is a quick-reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a search-box (geocoder) to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map**, together with the trade-offs you’ll need to consider (cost, licensing, self-hosting, UI look-and-feel, and how the code changes if you later migrate to MapLibre GL JS).

---

## 1️⃣ The “official” Mapbox way – `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Package** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` (npm) or the CDN `<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>` |
| **API** | Instantiates a `MapboxGeocoder` control that you add with `map.addControl(geocoder)`. |
| **Data source** | **Mapbox Geocoding API** – requires a **Mapbox access token** (public token is fine for client-side use). |
| **Pricing / limits** | Free tier: 100 k requests / month, 600 req / min per token. Paid plans give higher limits. |
| **Styling** | Comes with its own CSS (`mapbox-gl-geocoder.css`). You can override the `.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder` class or supply a custom `placeholder`, `proximity`, `bbox`, etc. |
| **Pros** | • Tight integration (auto-focus, result biasing, language support). <br>• Works out-of-the-box with Mapbox GL JS v2 (no code changes). |
| **Cons** | • Ties you to Mapbox’s commercial geocoding service (token, usage limits). <br>• If you ever want to move to MapLibre you’ll have to swap the plugin (see section 5). |
| **Installation** | ```bash\nnpm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder\n```\n```js\nimport MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';\nimport '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';\n\nconst geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({\n  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken, // <-- your Mapbox token\n  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,                // <-- required for the built-in UI\n  placeholder: 'Search places…',\n  proximity: {longitude: -122.42, latitude: 37.78}, // optional bias\n});\nmap.addControl(geocoder);\n``` |

> **Tip:** If you only need forward-geocoding (address → coordinates) you can also call the REST endpoint directly (`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/<query>.json?...`) and build your own UI. The official plugin just wraps that call.

---

## 2️⃣ Open-source / self-hosted alternatives (no Mapbox token)

If you want to avoid Mapbox’s commercial service—or you’re already planning to move to MapLibre—pick one of the following free/open geocoding back-ends and pair it with a compatible UI control.

| Service | How to use it | npm UI package (if any) | License / cost | Typical usage notes |
|---------|---------------|------------------------|----------------|---------------------|
| **Nominatim** (OpenStreetMap) | Public demo (`https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?...`) **or** self-hosted (Docker, Ubuntu, etc.) | `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (works with any endpoint) or roll your own fetch + `<input>` | Free, but **must** show OSM attribution; public instance limited to low-volume (≈1 req /s). | Good for worldwide address search. Results include `display_name`, `lat`, `lon`. |
| **Photon** (Komoot) | Public demo (`https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q=…`) **or** self-hosted (Docker) | Same as above | Free, public instance rate-limited; self-hosted is free. | Faster than Nominatim for autocomplete; returns `features` array compatible with GeoJSON. |
| **Pelias** | Self-hosted (Docker-compose) or use a hosted provider (e.g., Mapzen, MapTiler). | Same as above | Open-source (MIT); hosting cost depends on your infra. | Very configurable (multiple data sources, language support). |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | Hosted SaaS (`https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/<query>.json?...`) | `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (just set `apiKey`) | Free tier 100 k req/mo, paid plans for higher volume. | Drop-in replacement for Mapbox Geocoder with the same UI. |
| **Algolia Places** (deprecated, but still works) | Hosted by Algolia (`https://places-dsn.algolia.net/1/places/query`) | `places.js` + custom control | Free tier 100 k req/mo, paid for higher. | Good for “type-ahead” UI, but not a full geocoder (no reverse). |
| **Custom UI + fetch** | Write a tiny `<input>` + debounce → `fetch(url).then(r=>r.json())` | No extra package needed | — | Gives you full control over request/response handling, UI, and can combine multiple services. |

### 2.1 Using the **MapLibre-compatible geocoder UI** (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`)

The MapLibre UI is a **fork of the Mapbox plugin** that removes the hard-coded Mapbox token requirement and lets you point it at any geocoding endpoint that follows the Mapbox Geocoding API shape (most open services provide a compatible response format, or you can adapt it with a tiny transformer).

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

// Example with the public Nominatim endpoint
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // The UI expects the same option names as the Mapbox version
  placeholder: 'Search…',
  // Nominatim does not need an access token, but we must tell the plugin where to send the request.
  // The plugin lets you override the request function:
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const params = new URLSearchParams({
      q: query,
      format: 'json',
      addressdetails: 1,
      limit: 5,
    });
    const response = await fetch(`https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?${params}`);
    const data = await response.json();

    // Convert Nominatim results to the Mapbox-style feature format
    return {
      features: data.map((place) => ({
        type: 'Feature',
        geometry: {
          type: 'Point',
          coordinates: [parseFloat(place.lon), parseFloat(place.lat)],
        },
        place_name: place.display_name,
        properties: place,
      })),
    };
  },
});

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

> **Why the `forwardGeocode` hook?**  
> The UI component only cares about a `features` array that follows the Mapbox Geocoding response shape. By providing a custom `forwardGeocode` (and optionally `reverseGeocode`) you can plug any service into the same UI.

### 2.2 Using **Nominatim** directly (no UI library)

If you prefer a minimal UI (e.g., a plain `<input>` with a dropdown you style yourself), you can skip the plugin entirely:

```html
<input id="search" type="text" placeholder="Search…" />
<ul id="results" style="position:absolute; background:#fff; list-style:none; margin:0; padding:0;"></ul>
```

```js
const input = document.getElementById('search');
const results = document.getElementById('results');
let timeout = null;

input.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  clearTimeout(timeout);
  const query = e.target.value.trim();
  if (!query) { results.innerHTML = ''; return; }

  timeout = setTimeout(async () => {
    const params = new URLSearchParams({
      q: query,
      format: 'json',
      addressdetails: 1,
      limit: 5,
    });
    const resp = await fetch(`https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?${params}`);
    const data = await resp.json();

    results.innerHTML = data.map(p => `
      <li data-lon="${p.lon}" data-lat="${p.lat}" style="padding:4px; cursor:pointer;">
        ${p.display_name}
      </li>`).join('');

    // click handler
    results.querySelectorAll('li').forEach(li => {
      li.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const lon = parseFloat(li.dataset.lon);
        const lat = parseFloat(li.dataset.lat);
        map.flyTo({center: [lon, lat], zoom: 14});
        results.innerHTML = '';
        input.value = li.textContent;
      });
    });
  }, 300); // debounce
});
```

> **Pros:** No extra dependencies, full control over markup.  
> **Cons:** You have to implement the dropdown, keyboard navigation, and any result-biasing yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ What to choose? Decision matrix

| Decision factor | **Mapbox Geocoder (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`)** | **Nominatim (public)** | **Self-hosted Nominatim / Photon / Pelias** | **MapTiler Geocoding** | **Custom UI + any API** |
|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------|
| **Do you already have a Mapbox token?** | ✅ Use it directly | ❌ Requires separate service | ✅ (no token needed) | ✅ (requires MapTiler API key) | ✅ |
| **Budget / commercial usage** | Free up to 100 k req/mo, then paid | Free (but must host yourself for production) | Free (hosting cost) | Free tier 100 k req/mo, then paid | Depends on chosen API |
| **Rate-limit tolerance** | 600 req/min per token (hard) | Public demo limited to ~1 req/s, IP-wide | Unlimited (your own infra) | 100 k/mo free, then tiered | Whatever your backend can handle |
| **Geocoding quality (global coverage, POI bias)** | Very good, includes Mapbox POI data
